# fixedbugs, defect 070, panels 168 to 173, closed 2026-09-21.
#
# SYMPTOM: `check` 0, `build` 0, and the program died inside `eat` with an
# EMPTY stderr and an exit code that was 133 on one run and 134 on another.
# The language's own report, `panic: 1 lease(s) never ended`, never printed,
# because C's `free` killed the process before `main` returned.
#
# CAUSE: a lease is a copy the program owns and `end_lease` frees; a C
# function that frees it frees the program's bytes through a pointer that was
# never `malloc`'s, and on this platform the allocator says nothing for any bad
# free (panel 172's completeness critic, five shapes in plain C, zero bytes).
# No word on a parameter could have said so here or anywhere: three of the
# defect's seven shapes free the lease through a function with no pointer
# parameter at all.
#
# FIX: the runtime names the live lease from the signal handler it already
# installs for stack exhaustion, reading the live-lease balance it already
# keeps (panel 172, resolution item 1; panel 173 judged the mechanism). The
# program still dies, because C freed memory it did not own; it dies saying so.
# 133 and 134 are two allocator paths (SIGTRAP, SIGABRT), so the expectation
# below pins the message and not the code.

extern "c-frees-a-lease-and-the-runtime-names-it.h"
    function eat(s: cstr)

function main()
    x = "payload"
    c: cstr @ x.lease()
    print(1)
    eat(s: c)
    end_lease(@c)
